  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Data and Displacement: Assessing the Practical and Ethical Implications of Targeting Humanitarian Protection</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">This project contributes to a new understanding of the ways that contemporary humanitarian risk is generated, resolved and/or perpetuated in situations of conflict and development, specifically by assessing protection interventions that are grounded in the use of largescale quantitative, biometric and visual data with internally displaced persons (IDPs). The coordination and operational distribution of humanitarian assistance increasingly relies on tools of data gathering and visualisation, with the IOM&#x2019;s Displacement Tracking Matrix (DTM) as a key database within a rapidly growing field of importance to the wider humanitarian community. Our research assesses the effectiveness of data-based responses to humanitarian risk, focusing in particular on the data-based targeting of humanitarian assistance in the African region through examining two key cases in which such interventions have been central: northern Nigeria and South Sudan. By juxtaposing data sources with an overview of coordination mechanisms and operational activities across each site, we assess the resonance between complex data and humanitarian assistance and highlight problems of interference that impact the transposition of data into practice. These areas of interference are further explored through qualitative research both with practitioners and local stakeholders, as well as with IDPs themselves as subjects who negotiate humanitarian assistance.</narrative>
  </description>
